      ":question/id": "4d345e50-4921-588f-ae74-039f20e6e137",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-100242",
      ":question/problem": "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{ex}{5})=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{ex}{5}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ex)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
