      ":question/id": "8b91269e-ac3e-5858-87ab-575f9e7b0df8",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-66781",
      ":question/problem": "${log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{27})=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{27}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5x)-{log}_{3}⁡(27) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{3}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
